    {
      "id": "JS1fO2wXo0fnMZIDXoEve",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -7229.754729652703,
      "y": -1.329192650559662,
      "width": 372.75,
      "height": 35,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffffff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "biL",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 895660611,
      "version": 132,
      "versionNonce": 411750752,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1789702258021,
      "created": 1789614764394,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Balanced + order preserved",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Balanced + order preserved",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "dEBiglA7khVC76sL4dUHN",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 6502.963369126979,
      "y": -110.77200221670978,
      "width": 851.9000244140625,
      "height": 700,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffffff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "biX",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 99284899,
      "version": 1470,
      "versionNonce": 218671456,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1789705212423,
      "created": 1789616109086,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Rotations are a *very* powerful primitive for balancing trees.\nrotations used by:\n - AVL trees\n - red black trees\n - splay trees\n - treaps\n - WAVL trees\n - weight-balanced trees\n - AA trees\n - etc.\n\nHow do we prove the above with certainty that:\n\"We can transform any arbitrary n-node binary search tree can\nbe transformed into any other\"\n\nProposition?\nCan transform any n-node BST into another \nn-node BST using just rotations!\n\nlet's prove it. ",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Rotations are a *very* powerful primitive for balancing trees.\nrotations used by:\n - AVL trees\n - red black trees\n - splay trees\n - treaps\n - WAVL trees\n - weight-balanced trees\n - AA trees\n - etc.\n\nHow do we prove the above with certainty that:\n\"We can transform any arbitrary n-node binary search tree can\nbe transformed into any other\"\n\nProposition?\nCan transform any n-node BST into another \nn-node BST using just rotations!\n\nlet's prove it. ",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "w4tbFM-I-FXsedvvI_pGw",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1653.0609298976715,
      "y": 8969.916597698035,
      "width": 1101,
      "height": 875,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffffff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "biY",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 703240585,
      "version": 237,
      "versionNonce": 945445024,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1789706917214,
      "created": 1789618588658,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": " - 13.1-2 Shows immediately why inserting a node as black causes a black-height\n   problem, while inserting red risks only the red-red property. Excellent\n   motivation for RB-INSERT.\n - 13.1-5 Probably the most important exercise in §13.1. It makes students\n   derive why RB trees cannot become arbitrarily skewed: longest path ≤ twice\n   shortest path. This connects properties 4 and 5 directly to balance.\n - 13.2-3 Best exercise for understanding what a rotation physically does: one\n   region gets deeper, one stays put, one gets shallower.\n - 13.2-4 Best exercise for understanding why rotations are powerful: any BST\n   shape can be transformed into any other using only \\(O(n)\\) rotations while\n   preserving order.\n - 13.3-1 Answers the crucial question: why insert red? Because black would\n   immediately disturb black height.\n - 13.3-2 Walk through an entire insertion sequence. This turns the fixup cases\n   from abstract diagrams into something concrete.\n - 13.3-3 Extremely important for the way you're thinking about teaching it:\n   explicitly verify that the insertion transformations preserve property 5 /\n   black height.\n - 13.4-1 In the 4th edition: understand why deleting a red node doesn't disturb\n   black heights. This establishes why deleting black is the difficult case.\n - 13.4-4 Do the full deletion sequence. Deletion is difficult enough that\n   students need an actual trace rather than only pseudocode.\n - 13.4-6 Perhaps the single best deletion exercise: count black nodes through\n   every fixup transformation and verify the black-height relationship. This\n   explains why the bizarre-looking deletion cases work.",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": " - 13.1-2 Shows immediately why inserting a node as black causes a black-height\n   problem, while inserting red risks only the red-red property. Excellent\n   motivation for RB-INSERT.\n - 13.1-5 Probably the most important exercise in §13.1. It makes students\n   derive why RB trees cannot become arbitrarily skewed: longest path ≤ twice\n   shortest path. This connects properties 4 and 5 directly to balance.\n - 13.2-3 Best exercise for understanding what a rotation physically does: one\n   region gets deeper, one stays put, one gets shallower.\n - 13.2-4 Best exercise for understanding why rotations are powerful: any BST\n   shape can be transformed into any other using only \\(O(n)\\) rotations while\n   preserving order.\n - 13.3-1 Answers the crucial question: why insert red? Because black would\n   immediately disturb black height.\n - 13.3-2 Walk through an entire insertion sequence. This turns the fixup cases\n   from abstract diagrams into something concrete.\n - 13.3-3 Extremely important for the way you're thinking about teaching it:\n   explicitly verify that the insertion transformations preserve property 5 /\n   black height.\n - 13.4-1 In the 4th edition: understand why deleting a red node doesn't disturb\n   black heights. This establishes why deleting black is the difficult case.\n - 13.4-4 Do the full deletion sequence. Deletion is difficult enough that\n   students need an actual trace rather than only pseudocode.\n - 13.4-6 Perhaps the single best deletion exercise: count black nodes through\n   every fixup transformation and verify the black-height relationship. This\n   explains why the bizarre-looking deletion cases work.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "f4sISecTqfiy4pGS44Pnq",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1751.4313997327404,
      "y": 9963.448645514716,
      "width": 876.4249877929688,
      "height": 35,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffffff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
