      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "(ask audience) how would we turn this into a right leaning tree? ",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "291kIW54Cgmen2kSEGet6",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 10777.942754326317,
      "y": 96.89144381279851,
      "width": 636.1749877929688,
      "height": 300,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 4,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "c03J",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1842996384,
      "version": 193,
      "versionNonce": 882065760,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1789704927416,
      "created": 1789702434309,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "While a node on the right spine has a left child, right-rotate \nthat node. The left child joins the spine, increasing its length \nby one. If some node is not yet on the spine, some spine node\nmust have a left child,  so we can continue. Therefore eventually\nall (n) nodes lie on the  spine. \n\nNOTE: each rotation in this algorithm increases right-spine \nlength by 1. so at most n-1 rotations are required.\n\n\nwith this algorithm we can basically turn any BST into a right\nleaning tree",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "While a node on the right spine has a left child, right-rotate \nthat node. The left child joins the spine, increasing its length \nby one. If some node is not yet on the spine, some spine node\nmust have a left child,  so we can continue. Therefore eventually\nall (n) nodes lie on the  spine. \n\nNOTE: each rotation in this algorithm increases right-spine \nlength by 1. so at most n-1 rotations are required.\n\n\nwith this algorithm we can basically turn any BST into a right\nleaning tree",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "vTcq5Q5spyTbyxmiJkIQA",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 8645.316749843718,
      "y": 443.03704729450214,
      "width": 662.625,
      "height": 750,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 4,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "c03K",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 2144370848,
      "version": 239,
      "versionNonce": 1306738848,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1789706679334,
      "created": 1789702818554,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Start with the tree and ask something like:\n\n“How could we make this look more like a right-going chain?”\n\nThen draw attention to the current right spine and ask \nthem to count its length.  If they don't immediately see the\nrotation strategy, give the key observation:\n\n“Look at a node on the spine with a left child. Is there a rotation \nthat would put that left child onto the spine?”\n\nOnce they see one right rotation do that, ask again:\n\n“What happened to the spine length?”\n\nNow they have the crucial invariant/progress measure:\n\nk -> k+1\n\nAt that point you can give them the algorithm explicitly:\n\nRepeatedly find a node on the right spine with a left child \nand right-rotate it.\n\nAnd then immediately ask:\n\n“Why can this never get stuck before every node is on the spine?”\n\n\"If some node is not yet on the spine, some spine node\nmust have a left child, \"",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Start with the tree and ask something like:\n\n“How could we make this look more like a right-going chain?”\n\nThen draw attention to the current right spine and ask \nthem to count its length.  If they don't immediately see the\nrotation strategy, give the key observation:\n\n“Look at a node on the spine with a left child. Is there a rotation \nthat would put that left child onto the spine?”\n\nOnce they see one right rotation do that, ask again:\n\n“What happened to the spine length?”\n\nNow they have the crucial invariant/progress measure:\n\nk -> k+1\n\nAt that point you can give them the algorithm explicitly:\n\nRepeatedly find a node on the right spine with a left child \nand right-rotate it.\n\nAnd then immediately ask:\n\n“Why can this never get stuck before every node is on the spine?”\n\n\"If some node is not yet on the spine, some spine node\nmust have a left child, \"",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "nkRTmQR-kXaA-GCsBMPE0",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 9352.690045755226,
      "y": 516.2050383798274,
      "width": 659.875,
      "height": 475,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 4,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "c03Q",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 813590880,
      "version": 644,
      "versionNonce": 1063681376,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1789704911031,
      "created": 1789702891179,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "THE ALGORITHM:\n\nWhile a node on the right spine has a left child, right-rotate \nthat node. The left child joins the spine, increasing its length \nby one. If some node is not yet on the spine, some spine node\nmust have a left child,  so we can continue. Therefore eventually\nall (n) nodes lie on the  spine.  \n\nIn fact, suppose if the initial spine has \"s\" nodes and \"n\" \nis the total number of nodes. \n\nSince each rotations adds one node to the spine, we can say that \nn - s <= n - 1\nrotations suffice.\n\nat most we take n - 1 rotations to make a \n\nwith this algorithm we can basically turn any BST into a right\nleaning tree",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "THE ALGORITHM:\n\nWhile a node on the right spine has a left child, right-rotate \nthat node. The left child joins the spine, increasing its length \nby one. If some node is not yet on the spine, some spine node\nmust have a left child,  so we can continue. Therefore eventually\nall (n) nodes lie on the  spine.  \n\nIn fact, suppose if the initial spine has \"s\" nodes and \"n\" \nis the total number of nodes. \n\nSince each rotations adds one node to the spine, we can say that \nn - s <= n - 1\nrotations suffice.\n\nat most we take n - 1 rotations to make a \n\nwith this algorithm we can basically turn any BST into a right\nleaning tree",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
