      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1. BST property and inorder ordering. Make absolutely clear that for every node,\n   keys in the left subtree are smaller and keys in the right subtree are\n   larger. This is the invariant rotations must preserve.\n2. BST insertion. This is simple and directly sets up RB-INSERT: descend to a\n   NIL position and attach the new node there.\n3. Minimum / successor just enough for deletion. Students need to understand\n   why, when deleting a node with two children, we use its successor—usually the\n   minimum of its right subtree.\n4. TRANSPLANT. Definitely worth covering if you're doing deletion.\n5. BST deletion and its three structural cases.\n6. Then introduce RB insertion/deletion as the same structural operations plus\n   additional bookkeeping and fixup.",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1. BST property and inorder ordering. Make absolutely clear that for every node,\n   keys in the left subtree are smaller and keys in the right subtree are\n   larger. This is the invariant rotations must preserve.\n2. BST insertion. This is simple and directly sets up RB-INSERT: descend to a\n   NIL position and attach the new node there.\n3. Minimum / successor just enough for deletion. Students need to understand\n   why, when deleting a node with two children, we use its successor—usually the\n   minimum of its right subtree.\n4. TRANSPLANT. Definitely worth covering if you're doing deletion.\n5. BST deletion and its three structural cases.\n6. Then introduce RB insertion/deletion as the same structural operations plus\n   additional bookkeeping and fixup.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "ZE-tJlAsIzM44_9ssH2T7",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1834.0421536313006,
      "y": -9993.384149272331,
      "width": 741.2159534648536,
      "height": 210,
      "angle": 0,
